dArkOS: ES reads the backlight level from sysfs, not through brightnessctl.
BrightnessInfoComponent (the brightness popup) asks DisplayPanelControl for the
level every 40 ms while ES is on screen, and with brightnessctl installed each
call went through popen(): a shell, brightnessctl and awk, 20 times a second.
On the RG52 Mini, ES idle in the system list, those took 19.8% of a core (30 s,
2026-11-08). The function already read /sys/class/backlight/backlight/
{brightness,max_brightness} when brightnessctl is missing, and rounds the
percent as `brightnessctl -m` does; now it reads them whenever they exist, and
brightnessctl stays for a device without them. Setting the level still goes
through brightnessctl. ES idle, A B A B of 30 s each: the whole system 56.5%
of a core before, 36.3% with this; the CPU's mean clock 774 MHz, 719 MHz.

--- a/es-core/src/DisplayPanelControl.cpp
+++ b/es-core/src/DisplayPanelControl.cpp
@@ -97,11 +97,18 @@
 
 int DisplayPanelControl::getBrightnessLevel()
 {
-	if (mExistBrightnessctl)
+	// dArkOS: the backlight's own files first, brightnessctl only without them.
+	// BrightnessInfoComponent asks for the level every 40 ms while ES is on
+	// screen, and through brightnessctl each call started a shell, brightnessctl
+	// and awk: a fifth of a core on the RG52 Mini with ES idle. brightnessctl -m
+	// rounds its percent as the code below does.
+	static const bool sysfs = Utils::FileSystem::exists(BACKLIGHT_BRIGHTNESS_NAME) &&
+		Utils::FileSystem::exists(BACKLIGHT_BRIGHTNESS_MAX_NAME);
+	if (mExistBrightnessctl && !sysfs)
 		return std::atoi( getShOutput(std::string(BRIGHTNESSCTL_PATH).append(" -m | awk -F',|%' '{print $4}'")).c_str() );
 
 
-	int value,
+	int value = 0,
 			fd,
 			max = 255;
 	char buffer[BACKLIGHT_BUFFER_SIZE + 1];
